export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>I still remember my many attempts at getting into <em>Monster Hunter 1</em> on the PS2 when I was still in elementary school. The countless hours I spent on <em>Kingdom Hearts 2</em> and <em>God of War</em> prevented me from enjoying a more methodical and slow-paced combat model. My 10-year-old self was <em>pretty</em> impulsive.</p>\n<p>My biggest gripe with the game was, surprisingly, its use of the right analog stick; pushing the stick forward to attack is an odd choice to me in retrospect.</p>\n<p>Maybe I didn’t have the patience to discover its brilliance. Maybe it was just unfortunate for me and questionable for the game.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/5272b2751608299ce9ea.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/5272b2751608299ce9ea.webp\" width=\"961\" height=\"718\" style=\"--source-width:295px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This is me running away from the right analog stick.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/46c0005d6016b636623b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/46c0005d6016b636623b.webp\" width=\"1036\" height=\"580\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Of course, I never paid attention to this series afterward. And at the same time, it was gradually moving to the company behind the Switch portable devices, which I barely had any interest in.</p>\n<p>At E3 2017, the developer announced Monster Hunter: World for PS4, Xbox One, and PC. The subtitle World not only marked a drastic change to a more open design but also implied the development team’s aspiration to bring this franchise to the international audience. As a matter of fact, Monster Hunter was relatively unknown beyond the land of Japan.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Over a year after its release, <em>Monster Hunter: World</em> received a major expansion under the name <em>Iceborne</em>. This DLC rekindled my passing interest in the game, and I decided to finally form a team for monster hunting. Only then did I realize that the developer has successfully brought the series back to old fans in full glory, and introduced it to the newer audience spectacularly.</p>\n<p><em>Monster Hunter: World</em> is an experience second to none.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a9a17c9fe8262c66fa55.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a9a17c9fe8262c66fa55.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"642\" style=\"--source-width:295px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/2edab835c15a09987a47.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/2edab835c15a09987a47.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/2cfc09f6268ab03c37f1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/2cfc09f6268ab03c37f1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Both games emphasize stamina management and require the player to understand what kind of threat he is facing.</em></p>\n<p><em> However, the line between these two games only starts to blur in the epic boss battles; the Souls experience leans toward smaller encounters for the majority of its playtime.</em></p></td><td><p>I used to hear that sentence over and over from the series fans, but I’ve never expected to say it myself. That being said, the hyperbole is not without merits. Looking at the ever-expanding library of our industry, you can easily reference one title to another.</p>\n<p>“If you enjoy…, then you should play…” is what we’ve been telling each other for years. But for years, we have <em>not </em>been told what to play if we enjoyed <em>Monster Hunter</em> despite its remnants of brilliance in many games we love ─ like Dark Souls.</p>\n<p>We, of course, cannot say that <em>Dark Souls</em> copied or took inspirations directly from <em>Monster Hunter</em>. However, the similarities in terms of design are a true testament to Monster Hunter’s greatness. With <em>Dark Souls</em> being this successful and influential, I think there is definitely a lot to learn from <em>Monster Hunter</em>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>One of the running themes in long-running IPs ─ not just in the game industry ─ is the need to reinvent and keep things fresh for their audience. After all, there was a reason why <em>Resident Evil</em> is now playable in three different camera perspectives. Oddly enough, Monster Hunter is amongst the few cases that require no significant changes to its core design, which consists of:</p>\n<ul><li>Action</li><li>Multiplayer</li><li>Customization</li></ul>\n<p>The core gameplay loop has not changed for over a decade: fight monsters, loot their remainings, and craft better equipment. The simplicity of this concept is far from innovative, but <em>Diablo</em> has proven that this formula is ceaslessly engaging. The excitement of making builds and growing stronger never gets old in these games.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/70aa650c123af0ca4380.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/70aa650c123af0ca4380.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:295px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/797fbee33d3ebcc4cc3b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/797fbee33d3ebcc4cc3b.webp\" width=\"737\" height=\"414\" style=\"--source-width:295px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/392f3bdb053f7c4fe14a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/392f3bdb053f7c4fe14a.webp\" width=\"1139\" height=\"639\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/321ba6117e2e357c8a32.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/321ba6117e2e357c8a32.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"650\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>With such a refined system already in place, <em>World</em> didn’t have to reinvent the wheel for a genre it exclusively owns. It, instead, went on to adapt the now-ubiquitous design trend: open-world. While the game isn’t exactly a sandbox, its environments certainly gain more square meters and play a bigger role than ever before.</p>\n<p>“You spend so much time with the boss battles in <em>Monster Hunter</em>, so we tried to utilize a broad number of methods to have a good variety, and keep a good tempo going,” the game director shared. “This is how we were able to realize <em>Monster Hunter: World</em>’s most ambitious change: to make it possible to use the environment.”</p>\n<p>This is most evident in the starting area: The Ancient Forest. This place is a feast for the eye, containing a myriad of minute details filling up your screen with every pixel rendered. In regard to level design, it is a masterclass.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Personally, I never once felt immersed in this forest as I have never been to a real forest myself. However, I have been to a similarly designed space before ─ the police station in Resident Evil 2. Just like the RPD, you will return and explore the Ancient Forest many times with enemies roaming in every corner and items littered everywhere. Over time, you will master all the nooks and crannies in these places, which allows for more intentional gameplay. That moment when you know exactly where to go and what to do is when the real fun begins.</p>\n<p>There is one huge difference between these two games, though. In <em>Monster Hunter: World</em>, you are the hunter, not the hunted; therefore, the level design has to take that aspect into consideration.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/2e208ace57ce30449269.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/2e208ace57ce30449269.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"359\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><br><em>The more you play, the more familiar the area becomes. Eventually, you will remember the exact enemy and item placements, which provides a long-term benefit.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Movements are important in every game; diversified movement options are particularly important in open-world games because variety can help ease the bore of inaction. Grand Theft Auto games possess a huge collection of vehicles for a reason.</p>\n<p><em>Monster Hunter: World</em> approaches traversal mechanics in response to the environments. Your basic tool set only consists of walking and running. But depending on the context, you can perform different maneuvers, ranging from swinging, climbing, crouching to swimming. Everything is bound to a specific element in the wild.</p>\n<p>With this many options, players can opt for their preferred methods to track monsters down, resulting in a more personal journey.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a3f4d03985c21dfca991.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a3f4d03985c21dfca991.webp\" width=\"720\" height=\"821\" style=\"--source-width:295px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The variety of traversal options makes moving around less monotonous.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/60dbd317c6e0c6e745fe.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/60dbd317c6e0c6e745fe.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/e426d317074f8140c2d3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/e426d317074f8140c2d3.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>The Ancient Forest is as high as it is wide. By having a combination of stooping cliffs and meandering trees, this area offers many vantage points for combat and exploration and evokes a strong sense of realism. Real forests are layered.</p>\n<p>Even though not everyone is an ecological expert, everyone knows how a forest feel like, especially when it comes to flora and fauna. Certain types of animals and plants only appear in certain places. Therefore, when a player goes upward to the emergent layer, he will automatically expect a different kind of monster compared to the lower layers.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Tobi-Kadachi delves in section 14 of the Ancient Forest ─ an area with great height and many tall trees. This creature isn’t reliant on its size and brute force to dominate its enemies; it is deadly due to speed and agility. By weaving in and out of nearby trees, and gliding toward its target from above, Tobi-Kadachi keeps the player constantly moving to dodge and wait for an opening.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/46d002acf43c1155a3fe.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/46d002acf43c1155a3fe.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:295px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>On the other hand, Anjanath relies entirely on its size, and strength, and fire breath, making it truly terrifying in narrow spaces. Therefore, luring it out to a more open area is a recommended strategy.</p>\n<p>It will still be very deadly, but at least you can run now</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/d1ad7ea8fbf41cff73c4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/d1ad7ea8fbf41cff73c4.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"320\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/34ce276db76e37dbdce5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/34ce276db76e37dbdce5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"859\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/5bb627611a0c35d40a9e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/5bb627611a0c35d40a9e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"651\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>The previous two examples show how the environments benefit the predator and the prey. But the most exciting part of the game is the interactions of these monsters.</p>\n<p>For example, the Great Jargas will flee at a certain health threshold. If it stumbles across an Aptonoth, it will consume the animal and temporarily gain strength; its moveset will also differ. In turn, this giant lizard could be a prey for the fire-breathing Anjanath. So on and so forth.</p>\n<p>The random nature of these events lends the game a strong sense of realism despite its stylized graphics. Moreover, this creates opportunities for players to pitch two monsters against each other, and take advantage of being a third-party</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>As dynamic as the giant monsters are, the forest would feel lifeless if the smaller elements were static. There is a wide array of environmental hazards such as flashfly, poison cup, vine trap, and dam. These situational tools also have fixed locations, so you either actively look for them or <em>improvise</em> in the heat of the battle. I find the latter to be more satisfying.</p>\n<p>My favorite is actually this little guy called <em>Paratoad</em>. Once killed, he’ll release a paralysis smoke cloud, which can temporarily disable even the biggest of monsters. Just make sure that you don’t get caught in it.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/4b95781ad94e94078ed9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/4b95781ad94e94078ed9.webp\" width=\"918\" height=\"457\" style=\"--source-width:295px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/e35ab0e01e7e131513ca.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/e35ab0e01e7e131513ca.webp\" width=\"896\" height=\"501\" style=\"--source-width:295px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>See you in the Ancient Forest</strong></p>\n<p>There are still other intricacies that the game has to offer, but I think this is enough for now. If I had to sell this game to a person in one sentence, I would say, “It allows you to make meaningful choices at any given moment.” Along with <em>Breath of the Wild</em> and <em>Marvel’s Spider-Man</em> on PS4, I think this is one of the few open-world cases where I prefer moving on foot to using fast travel. The levels have just the right amount of footprint while integrating plenty of unique interactions, both in and outside of combat. It hardly ever feels dull going from place to place.</p>\n<p>At its core, <em>Monster Hunter: World</em> is just about killing dragons and leveling up. It’s simple, fun stuff that everyone can enjoy. And I don’t think there’s anything wrong with that. Ultimately, video games are to <em>entertain</em>.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a0b8f34de7fc0e6495b1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a0b8f34de7fc0e6495b1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"636\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>";
export const images = ["assets/cookies-inline/2cfc09f6268ab03c37f1.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/2e208ace57ce30449269.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/2edab835c15a09987a47.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/321ba6117e2e357c8a32.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/34ce276db76e37dbdce5.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/392f3bdb053f7c4fe14a.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/46c0005d6016b636623b.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/46d002acf43c1155a3fe.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/4b95781ad94e94078ed9.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/5272b2751608299ce9ea.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/5bb627611a0c35d40a9e.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/60dbd317c6e0c6e745fe.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/70aa650c123af0ca4380.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/797fbee33d3ebcc4cc3b.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/a0b8f34de7fc0e6495b1.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/a3f4d03985c21dfca991.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/a9a17c9fe8262c66fa55.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/d1ad7ea8fbf41cff73c4.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/e35ab0e01e7e131513ca.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/e426d317074f8140c2d3.webp"];
